    librt: correct winding for explicit DSP B-C cell cuts
    
    The explicit ULlr permutation inverted both terrain triangle normals.
    The cell intersection state machine uses normal direction to classify
    entry and exit, so top-to-side rays could disappear.
    
    An independent triangle mesh and the DSP received the same 500,000
    Crofton chords. The mesh returned 3,689 hit rays, while DSP returned
    3,463; the DSP surface estimate was 10.35% below the exact area.
    Using the adaptive B-C permutation for explicit cuts preserves the
    selected diagonal and restores outward top normals. DSP then returned
    3,688 hit rays and the area error fell to 4.53%.
    
    Add a fixed top-to-side ray that checks entry and exit distances plus
    the top normal.

commit d729a7d3f2f9ee83f47aaffb37c849ee03aa6932
Author: Clifford Yapp <238416+starseeker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Sep 4 20:45:38 2026 +0000

    librt: make Crofton sampling deterministic and reusable
    
    Retain ordinary Monte Carlo chord sampling while making its stream
    explicit and portable. Draw independent sphere endpoints from a
    fixed-seed mt19937_64 generator so every setup is reproducible
    without changing the estimator's statistical convergence model.
    
    Convert the generator's high 53 bits explicitly to avoid library-
    specific real-number conversions. Consume four values per ray so
    ray offsets reproduce disjoint continuations of the same stream.
    
    Reuse a bounded C++ worker pool and stop only after windowed area
    estimates remain stable. This avoids repeated thread construction
    while retaining the existing stopping criteria.
    
    Add optional collection of ray identifiers, paired entry and exit
    hit points, normals, and partition thickness. This gives
    reconstruction callers structured source evidence while preserving
    lightweight area and volume estimation. Link librt to the thread
    target and extend the Crofton tests to verify that offset calls
    exactly reproduce the uninterrupted stream.

