    Enable RPP tests for seven bounded primitives
    
    Particle entered the function table with ft_use_rpp=0 in 1990
    (b85ddfc9ff4); RPC, RHC, EPA, and EHY did so in 1992
    (16dc889cfb5).  CLINE arrived with zero in 2000 (4a69d5ab3cb).
    ANNOT was a zero-flagged stub in 2012 (f31b27d992) and retained the
    value when its callbacks arrived in 2017 (cb83958bf58).  The later
    table moves and callback rewrites preserved these values.  The
    inspected commits record no per-primitive correctness or performance
    reason for choosing zero.
    
    The finite shot geometry is enclosed by the prepared boxes: particle
    uses its two end radii, CLINE includes its radius and beam expansion,
    and RPC, RHC, EPA, and EHY use conservative profile envelopes.
    Model-space ANNOT prep produces a BoT and uses its bounds; screen-space
    ANNOT has no shot geometry.  Enable the solid RPP check for these seven.
    
    Leave HALF at zero because it is infinite and SKETCH because it never
    shoots.  HF computes its box from the maximum height but omits a
    negative minimum, so its box can be too small.  ARB8 and ARBN already
    clip rays against their faces or planes, and SPH has a cheap quadratic
    shot; an added box test needs its own tolerance and performance case.
    BoT uses a BVH and origin backout, so changing its RPP behavior needs
    separate validation.  The remaining zero entries do not shoot rays.
    
    Five deterministic 1024-square primitives.g renders averaged 1.222
    seconds of shot time before and 1.166 after, with 178,709 fewer solid
    shot calls and identical pixels.  Two other camera views also matched,
    as did NIRT results for rays starting inside each of the six finite
    solid types.  The annotation raytrace regression passes.

