commit 72ec941830ccfa5f28c5220e411ef30d3833c054
Author: Clifford Yapp <238416+starseeker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 23:17:02 2026 -0400

    libdm: fix X embedded framebuffer and font metric handling
    
    Make the X24 embedded framebuffer follow the X display manager's current
    backing pixmap rather than retaining a copy of its X resource
    identifier.
    
    The X display manager uses a pixmap as its back buffer.  Whenever its
    Tk window is configured at a new size, X_configureWin_guts frees that
    pixmap and creates a replacement.  The embedded X24 framebuffer,
    however, received the pixmap identifier by value when it was opened
    and retained that value in xi_win.  It was never notified when the
    display manager replaced the pixmap.
    
    Consequently, after a ConfigureNotify the two components could have
    different views of the backing drawable:
    
        display manager: newly allocated pixmap
        X24 framebuffer: identifier of the freed pixmap
    
    The next framebuffer refresh would issue XPutImage against the stale
    identifier.  X protocol errors are asynchronous, so the invalid
    request was generally reported later by XFlush, XSync, or event queue
    processing.  Xlib's default error handler terminates the process with
    exit status 1.  Since graphical MGED redirects its diagnostics through
    the GUI, this appeared to users as an immediate, silent exit rather
    than an ordinary crash or Tcl error.  A common reproducer was to draw
    all.g and open the Raytrace Control Panel, which activates and refreshes
    the embedded framebuffer after Tk geometry processing has replaced the
    display manager pixmap.
    
    This is a longstanding ownership bug rather than an operation that
    suddenly became invalid with the upgrade to Tcl/Tk 8.6.
    
    The embedded-existing-resource path dates to January 1998
    (0b2e440288 and bf179a0e27).  It passed the display manager pixmap into
    X24 by value, and that behavior was preserved when framebuffer
    ownership was moved behind the display-manager API in 2014
    (8a97c2184a).
    
    Tk 8.5 happened to conceal the lifetime error.  Its Unix XID allocator
    intercepted Xlib resource allocation and kept freed identifiers on a
    LIFO stack.  X_configureWin_guts calls Tk_FreePixmap immediately before
    Tk_GetPixmap, so the replacement pixmap would normally receive the
    same numeric XID as the pixmap just freed.  Although X24 retained a
    stale value, that value accidentally referred to the newly created
    pixmap by the time it was used.
    
    Tk 8.6 removed that private allocator and made Tk_FreeXId a no-op,
    relying on the X server's XC-MISC resource management instead.  The
    replacement pixmap was therefore no longer likely to receive the same
    identifier.  This exposed the stale drawable retained by X24.  The
    problem was observed immediately after the 8.6 migration and recorded
    in 388a8afd6a on April 17, 2020 as an X error during fb_refresh and
    X24_blit.
    
    Pass X24 a const pointer to the display manager's pixmap slot and
    resolve that pointer whenever X24 draws.  Embedded framebuffer blits,
    region clears, and expose refreshes now always target the currently
    owned display-manager back buffer.  Standalone X24 framebuffers
    continue to use their own xi_win drawable.  The display manager remains
    responsible for copying the completed back buffer to the Tk window.
    
    Also centralize X11 font-width lookup for the X and OpenGL display
    managers.  Xlib permits XFontStruct::per_char to be NULL when all
    glyphs have identical bounds.  The previous configuration code
    dereferenced per_char unconditionally while selecting a font size.
    Use per_char->width when per-character metrics exist and
    min_bounds.width otherwise, avoiding a legal NULL dereference and
    keeping the two X11 backends consistent.
    
    Verified by rebuilding MGED, drawing moss.g/all.g, repeatedly opening
    the Raytrace Control Panel, resizing the graphics window before and
    after framebuffer writes, and completing a full-window embedded
    raytrace.  MGED remained running and framebuffer content followed the
    resized display-manager pixmap correctly.

