commit edd3614106cc7db7fc69c2e0f79a92dd70b6df7c
Author: Clifford Yapp <238416+starseeker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 18:48:21 2026 -0400

    Fix bounds traversal after subtractor tree shaking
    
    Observed symptoms were rt_bound_internal: rt_bound_tree() failed
    messages during facetize of havoc - investigation traced it to an
    unintended side effect of the subtraction-pruning optimization commit
    7f165d038a.  That same commit also introduced several lifecycle
    problems.  We're addressing those here, as well as a few other issues
    the audit also uncovered that were older defects.
    
    Issues directly attributable to subtraction pruning:
    
     - Disjoint RHS soltabs were freed while other code still assumed
       every database leaf had a prepared soltab.
     - Newly created soltabs could remain in rti_new_solids after
       pruning freed them.
     - Callbacks and reg_all_unions were processed before pruning
       finalized the tree.
     - Piece/model metadata was calculated before pruning.
     - Touching and near-touching AABBs were pruned too aggressively.
    
    Pre-existing defects exposed or discovered during the work:
    
     - Dynamic path discovery stopped at region boundaries, so primitive
       edits below regions were already unsupported.
     - rt_find_paths had a header/implementation mismatch from 667998ce6d.
     - Re-prep could retain freed pointers in per-type acceleration tables.
     - Region/solid pointer arrays had incomplete or incorrect clearing.
     - Path allocations leaked.
     - Repeated piece-state numbering was an old known issue—the source
       contained a comment describing it since 2011.
    
    In more detail:
    
    rt_gettree may prune a disjoint subtraction operand and release its
    soltab, while rt_bound_internal subsequently traverses the original
    database tree.  The bounds walker unnecessarily visited that right
    operand even though subtraction bounds are determined entirely by the
    left operand, producing primitive-as-combination lookup warnings and
    failed bounds on models such as havoc.g.  Limit OP_SUBTRACT bounds
    traversal to the minuend and add an in-memory regression covering a
    region with a disjoint subtractor.
    
    We also make AABB pruning conservative at touching and near-touching
    boundaries by applying the ray distance tolerance.  Defer gettree
    callbacks until pruning has finalized each region tree, then rebuild
    union flags, model bounds, and piece-state indices from surviving
    geometry.  Also remove pruned soltabs from the dynamic-reprep pending
    list before freeing them.
    
    Find edited descendants through region combinations using cycle-safe
    database paths, invalidate affected prepared regions without relying on
    pruned soltabs, and rebuild complete region expressions.  Release
    prepared trees safely, compact region and solid arrays, restore reverse
    region associations and per-type acceleration tables, clean pending
    state on failures, and align rt_find_paths with the resource-free public
    API.
    
    Added testing to cover tolerance-separated, near-touching, touching, and
    overlapping subtractors; verify callbacks and derived metadata observe
    finalized trees; exercise repeated piece indexing; and confirm a
    previously pruned subtractor can be edited, fully re-prepped, and ray
    traced without stale soltab metadata.

