    docs: consolidate STEP conversion guidance
    
    Replace obsolete schema-specific converter manual pages with
    documentation for the unified step-g and g-step interfaces.  Put concise
    defaults and task-oriented options immediately after each synopsis, then
    move policy, schema, representation, metadata, and diagnostic detail to
    later sections.
    
    Centralize shared format coverage in STEP(5).  Organize it around
    supported application protocols, FILE_SCHEMA selection, geometry,
    assemblies, native CSG, AP242 PMI, metadata round trips, validation, and
    schema provenance.  Remove repeated entity-by-entity explanations where
    target-schema validation and reporting policy communicate the same
    contract more clearly.
    
    Document permissive import and export defaults together with strict,
    exact, no-repair, invalid-rejection, shell-filtering, reporting,
    work-budget, and native-CSG controls.  Retain the essential limitations:
    content is never silently approximated, native CSG is opt-in, evaluated
    BREP export is not yet available, and PMI export remains future work.
    
    Correct the retained-graph description: scalable metadata lives in the
    versioned _STEP_METADATA binary object, while _GLOBAL carries locators
    and only mirrors modest graphs for compatibility.  Continue to document
    the ISO and STEPmod schema sources, Recommended Practice 8 assembly
    patterns, and redistribution constraints.
    
    Update the object type reference for enhanced datum and annotation
    records.

commit 5f0547cc70393f944b2484f3e87cd6c98b401989
Author: Clifford Yapp <238416+starseeker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 6 20:55:27 2026 -0400

    STEP: Add support for AP203e2, AP214 and AP242
    
    This is a major rework of the step import and export framework in
    BRL-CAD (with corresponding changes on the upstream stepcode libraries
    and tooling as well.)  AP203 basic should also see improvements in
    success rates.
    
    The biggest user-facing changes:
    
    - add support for AP203e2, AP214, and AP242 editions 1 through 4
    - replace schema-specific tools with unified step-g and g-step hosts
    - add optional native CSG export and boolean-tree round trips
    - import AP242 PMI as native datum and annotation objects
    - add permissive bounded repair with strict and exact audit modes
    - import shells, wireframes, sweeps, tessellations, and geometric sets
    - implement Recommended Practice 8 assembly and hierarchy patterns
    
    Replace the schema-specific converter executables with one step-g
    importer and one g-step exporter.  Both hosts discover edition-specific
    modules through a stable plugin boundary and use STEPcode's compact
    generated API.  AP203, AP203e2, AP214, and AP242 editions 1 through 4
    share conversion machinery without repeatedly compiling large schema
    bindings.  Uses the new stepcode v2 API for more compact, faster
    building.
    
    Keep common import, export, product, geometry, unit, presentation, and
    reporting logic at the top level.  Isolate genuinely schema-dependent
    adapters under ap203, ap203e2, ap214, and ap242, and dispatch from
    normalized Part 21 FILE_SCHEMA identifiers and aliases.  Remove the
    obsolete directional schema converters and their duplicated EXPRESS
    sources.
    
    Make ordinary import permissive and repair-oriented.  Validate the
    supplied topology first, apply only bounded and measured repairs, and
    publish a clearly tagged invalid BREP when source contradictions prevent
    valid geometry from being constructed.  Retain strict, exact, no-repair,
    and reject-invalid modes for auditing workflows where accepting repaired
    or invalid data is unwanted.
    
    Strengthen BREP construction for periodic and singular surfaces, pcurve
    regeneration, coherent branch selection, seam relocation, collapsed
    poles, keyhole loops, shell topology, and locally inconsistent
    tolerances.  Add bounded work budgets, nested face scheduling,
    memory-aware spooling, cancellation, progress snapshots, selected-entity
    retries, and deterministic database writes so difficult geometry remains
    diagnosable and contained.
    
    Import faceted and advanced BREP solids, open shells and surface models,
    geometric sets and wireframes, swept solids, tessellated geometry,
    mapped items, replicas, transformations, and repeated representation
    content.  Allow users to omit shell-only geometry when an exchange file
    mixes construction surfaces with its intended solid model.
    
    Implement the applicable assembly and product practices from Recommended
    Practice 8, including the AP203 hierarchy forms, product-definition
    graphs, NAUO/CDSR relationships, mapped representations, and Part 21
    SCOPE handling.  Preserve product identity, configuration relationships,
    document metadata, materials, presentation styles, and validated unit
    assignments.
    
    Map applicable AP242 semantic and graphical PMI into native datum and
    annotation objects.  Preserve datum references and systems, dimensional
    and geometric tolerances, semantic labels, presentation geometry, text
    runs, styles, and associations with the represented product instead of
    reducing all PMI to unstructured database attributes.
    
    Extend g-step with shared assembly, configuration, material, style,
    unit, and metadata emitters.  Add opt-in native CSG export for exact
    AP203e2, AP214, and AP242 primitive and boolean mappings BRL-CAD can
    represent, including rectangular pyramids, while retaining BREP
    fallback for unsupported primitives.  Import supported native CSG trees
    back into BRL-CAD combinations so optional CSG round trips preserve
    boolean structure.
    
    We keep the entity-type census in the unified importer and add
    concise diagnostic aggregation, machine-readable reports, repair
    provenance, partial success exit states, and scalable metadata storage.
    Sample database builds accept useful partial STEP output only when the
    expected database was actually written.
    
    Add focused, topic-specific testing inputs for schema dispatch and
