commit a36e15719d21d28704c6c91ed4995231a5094ca0
Author: Clifford Yapp <238416+starseeker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 1 17:10:16 2026 -0400

    libbu: make lazy semaphore publication and parallel bookkeeping race-free
    
    bu_semaphore_acquire() and bu_semaphore_release() invoke
    bu_semaphore_init() on every operation.  The lazy-initialization design
    introduced in 2014 therefore deliberately used an unlocked
    bu_nsemaphores check as its normal fast path, avoiding acquisition of
    the global initialization mutex for every semaphore operation.
    
    That fast path was operationally useful but was not valid under the C
    memory model.  It read bu_nsemaphores without synchronization while
    initializing threads wrote the same ordinary object under bu_init_lock.
    A lock held only by writers does not synchronize unlocked readers, so
    this was a data race and undefined behavior.
    
    The count is also more than a range bound: it is the publication marker
    that tells a caller that the corresponding native mutexes and their
    magic values are ready for use.  Without acquire/release ordering, a
    reader could observe the enlarged count without being guaranteed to
    observe all of the initialization writes that preceded it on a weakly
    ordered architecture.
    
    The March 2026 monotonic-count fix prevented a late initialization
    request from lowering bu_nsemaphores and causing already-live mutexes
    to be reinitialized.  That addressed the observed FreeBSD EPERM
    failure, but it did not remove the underlying C data race or establish
    a happens-before relationship for fast-path readers.
    
    Make bu_nsemaphores an atomic publication counter:
    
    * Use an acquire load for the unlocked already-initialized fast path.
    * Re-read the count after acquiring bu_init_lock.
    * Initialize only the range above that serialized value.
    * Release-store the new count only after every mutex and magic value in
      the range has been initialized.
    * Use Windows Interlocked operations on native Windows for compatibility
      with MSVC versions lacking complete C11 stdatomic support.
    * Snapshot and reset the count atomically in the explicitly quiescent
      bu_semaphore_free() path.
    
    The initialization mutex continues to serialize writers.  If a reader
    observes an older, smaller count, it only enters the slow path and
    rechecks under that mutex.  If it observes a sufficient count, the
    acquire load guarantees visibility of the initialization writes
    published by the release store.  Consequently, no mutex can be used
    before initialization or initialized twice while the pool is live.
    
    This preserves the original performance design.  An already-initialized
    semaphore operation performs one atomic integer load and comparison and
    does not acquire the initialization mutex.  On x86-64 the acquire load
    compiles to an ordinary load instruction.  The extra serialized work is
    confined to the rare pool-growth path.  Windows Interlocked loads are
    stronger than strictly necessary but retain the lock-free fast path.
    
    ThreadSanitizer testing of this path exposed additional libbu races:
    
    * parallel_wait_for_slot() polled worker counters without the
      BU_SEM_THREAD lock used by writers.
    * parallel mapping slots were initialized and cleared partly outside
      the lock used by slot allocation.
    * unrelated top-level bu_parallel() callers shared mapping slot zero.
    * the ncpu == 1 shortcut released its caller's still-live worker ID.
    * failed native thread creation leaked reserved mapping IDs.
    * malloc's first-use bomb-failsafe flag was read and written by
      concurrent callers without synchronization.
    
    Synchronize mapping allocation, initialization, and release under
    BU_SEM_THREAD, take locked snapshots in the throttled wait path, and
    give each top-level bu_parallel() invocation its own parent context.
    Do not release a worker mapping from the single-thread shortcut, and
    return mappings when native thread creation fails.  Protect the
    bomb-failsafe initialization with the existing malloc semaphore, adding
    no new allocation fast-path lock.
    
    Also serialize the Solaris concurrency hint and replace the Windows
    WaitForMultipleObjects join with individual waits.  This removes the
    Windows handle-count limit and keeps successfully created handles
    contiguous when a CreateThread call fails.
    
    Add regression coverage that repeatedly frees and concurrently rebuilds
    the native semaphore range, starts concurrent top-level bu_parallel()
    calls, and deterministically checks that recursive single-thread calls
    cannot make a live worker ID available for reuse.  Clean an unrelated
    race from the existing semaphore test so sanitizer results describe
    library behavior rather than test bookkeeping.
    
    Document why the earlier monotonic counter change was necessary but
    insufficient, and record the acquire/release publication contract.
    
    bu_semaphore_free() remains explicitly quiescent-only.  Supporting
    concurrent destruction would require a separate lifetime protocol in
    which every acquisition participates and teardown waits for all users;
    it is outside the current numeric-ID API contract.

